INT. OFFICE PARKING LOT - LATE AFTERNOON

Rows of cars. A cloud of exhaust. An orange sunset hits the glass tower above. A corporate banner reads SYNERGY AWAITS.

AUDREY KOLLMAN (50s, VP of Operations, severe bun) sits in her sedan with the window down, a cardboard box in the passenger seat. Next to the car, her former assistant NATHANIEL PARK (20s, lanyard, nervous) holds a sad potted fern. In the next row, security guard BEAUREGARD "BO" LAFAYETTE (60s, reflective vest) leans on his golf cart. And PRUDENCE TRAN (40s, HR, cardigan) walks up, clipboard in hand.

PRUDENCE
Audrey. I'm sorry.

AUDREY
Don't be sorry. Be efficient.

NATHANIEL
I brought your fern.

AUDREY
Thank you, Nathaniel.

BO
Ma'am, is there anything I can carry?

AUDREY
No. Thank you, Beauregard.

BO
It's Bo.

AUDREY
Bo. Of course.

Nathaniel sets the fern on the roof of her car. Prudence flips a page on her clipboard.

PRUDENCE
There are a few forms.

AUDREY
There are always forms.

NATHANIEL
I can fill them out for you.

PRUDENCE
Nathaniel, no.

NATHANIEL
Sorry.

BO
(low)
It's a shame.

PRUDENCE
It's company policy.

BO
I know. It's still a shame.

Audrey starts the engine. It idles. She doesn't put the car in gear. The four of them stand in the long amber light, none of them moving.

AUDREY
Well. This has been a long twelve years.

NATHANIEL
It was an honour.

AUDREY
Was it?

NATHANIEL
It was a lot of things.

She laughs, a quick, surprised sound. Prudence smiles. Bo tips his cap. Audrey puts the car in gear, takes the fern from the roof, and sets it on the passenger seat on top of the box.

AUDREY (CONT'D)
Water it on Thursdays.

NATHANIEL
It's yours.

AUDREY
It was always yours.

She drives away. They watch her go, three small figures in the light.
